  "Area": "Unclassified",
  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "Suppose \\([a\\ b]\\) denotes the average of \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) and \\(\\{a\\ b\\ c\\}\\) denotes the average of \\(a,\\) \\(b,\\) and \\(c.\\) What is the value of the following expression? \\[\\{\\{1 \\ 1 \\ 0\\} \\ [0 \\ 1] \\ 0\\}\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{9}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{18}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{18}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The inner averages must be evaluated first because their values become entries of the outer average. Using sum divided by number of entries gives\n\\[\n\\{1\\ 1\\ 0\\}=\\frac{1+1+0}{3}=\\frac23,\n\\qquad [0\\ 1]=\\frac{0+1}{2}=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe outer braces average these two values and \\(0\\), so there are still three entries. Using a common denominator of \\(6\\), the expression equals\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac23+\\frac12+0}{3}\n=\\frac{7/6}{3}\n=\\frac7{18}.\n\\]\nThe value is \\(\\frac7{18}\\), choice D.",
